 "why_logistic": "Gradient boosting was tried on the same folds and did not do meaningfully better, so the simpler logistic model is served: every weight can be read, explained to a judge, and exported without scikit-learn.",
 "limitations": [
  "Postings show what employers ask for and offer, not what a person can do. Applying the model to someone's skill mix describes how similar postings are paid; it does not predict that person's salary.",
  "Only about a third of postings mention a skill from the app's 37-skill vocabulary, and a skill needs at least 50 postings to be used; skills below that (for example numpy, git, docker) are ignored by the model rather than guessed.",
  "Skills add only a small amount on top of experience for the pay question (compare 'exp_only' with 'logistic').",
  "Salary is an employer-stated band and 'high pay' means the 15 LPA band or above; the file has no dates, so drift over time cannot be checked.",
  "Repeated postings are kept in the same cross-validation fold; reported scores are out-of-fold.",
  "Most single-skill weights are too uncertain to interpret. Skill advice is shown only for skills whose weight has a bootstrap 95% interval that excludes zero."
 ],
